                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Holy mother of tactical space mayhem, Strike Suit Infinity looked at Strike Suit Zero and basically screamed, \n\n\"Move over, cupcake. I'm about to inject pure adrenaline directly into your eyeballs.\"\n\nThis game takes the absolute BEST part of Zero—the combat—and cranks that glorious &#$%! to eleven. No unnecessary padding, no boring downtime, just relentless, score-chasing space carnage that'll have you muttering, \"One more run...\" until the Sun files for overtime. The combat is so ridiculously satisfying that every enemy capital ship feels like it personally insulted your mother. You don't destroy them...You ERASE them. You turn them into expensive, high-resolution space confetti while your Strike Suit zips around like a caffeinated homicidal hummingbird armed with enough missiles to violate several intergalactic treaties. And can we talk about the visuals? Sweet Merciful Hailey's comet... The sheer beauty of space is enough to make you stop and admire the stars—...for approximately 0.7 seconds, before another gigantic battlecruiser shows up begging to get the ever-living &#$%! blasted out of it. Every explosion is eye candy. Every dogfight is pure dopamine. Every new high score feels like you've personally slapped Isaac Newton across the face with a plasma cannon. The arcade gameplay loop is more addictive than free pizza in a game developer's office. You tell yourself, \n\n\"Just one more attempt.\"\n\nThree hours later you're still chasing that perfect run while your chair has permanently fused with your spine. This isn't trying to be a realistic space simulator. It knows exactly what it is: A gloriously over-the-top arcade combat masterpiece that grabs your brain by the collar, screams \"LOCK ON, YOU MAGNIFICENT BASTARD!\", and refuses to let go.\n\nStrike Suit Infinity is visual spectacle, arcade perfection, and space combat dopamine distilled into one glorious package. Now excuse me while I go commit several completely justified war crimes against another fleet of oversized space refrigerators.",
